# What EAS Build is allowed to upload.
#
# The first attempt at an APK compressed to 2.2 GB and was refused for being
# over the 2.0 GB limit. EAS archives from the repository root, so a build of
# the phone app was carrying desktop/ (3.2 GB), web/ (2.3 GB) and dashboard/
# (0.6 GB) along with it — plus every node_modules in the tree. The app itself
# is about 5 MB.
#
# Written as "exclude everything, then name what is needed" rather than a list
# of things to leave out. That matters more here than it looks: a .easignore
# REPLACES .gitignore for this upload, so the moment this file exists,
# api/.env, .env.production, dashboard/.env.local and the rest stop being
# protected by being git-ignored. Those hold the database password, the B2
# keys, the SMTP credentials and the JWT secrets. Denying by default means a
# new package added to this repo cannot quietly become part of a phone build,
# and a new secret cannot ride along with it.
#
# node_modules is excluded on purpose: EAS installs from package.json and
# package-lock.json on the build server, which is also what makes the build
# reproducible rather than a copy of whatever this laptop happens to have.

# Everything at the root…
/*

# …except the app being built. eas.json, app.json, app.config.js and the
# source all live in here.
!/mobile

# Inside it, what the build server does not need or must not see.
mobile/node_modules/
mobile/.expo/
mobile/android/
mobile/ios/
mobile/*.log
mobile/dist/

# Values come from the `env` block of the chosen profile in eas.json, not from
# a file — the CLI says so as it starts: "Environment variables loaded from the
# 'preview' build profile 'env' configuration".
mobile/.env
mobile/.env.*
!mobile/.env.example
